ed textures don’t turn into a blocky mess. Stadia therefore streams at a **sustained high bitrate** (often 15–25 Mbps for 1080p), while YouTube 1080p can look good at 3–8 Mbps. ### 4. Frame rate - Most YouTube videos at 1080p are 24, 25, or 30 fps. - Stadia targets **60 fps** at 1080p (smoother gameplay). All else being equal, doubling the frame rate roughly doubles the data – though it’s not the main culprit; the real-time encoding and latency constraints have a bigger impact. ### 5. Audio and other data - Stadia also streams high‑quality, low‑latency audio and constantly sends your controller inputs back. While not huge by itself, every millisecond matters, and the entire pipeline is optimised for real‑time performance, not data saving. **In short:** YouTube trades off a little buffering time for extreme compression efficiency. Stadia trades off data efficiency for instantaneous responsiveness and stable image quality – and that requires a much fatter pipe.
